00:09:50.210 --> 00:09:59.500
Keith Jansa: There was, again, like I said, no insurmountable barriers, and there was alignment with respect to this model law.

59
00:10:00.210 --> 00:10:10.740
Keith Jansa: Now, what was quite clear with respect to interoperability, between the jurisdictions is that there is a strategic imperative here.

60
00:10:10.740 --> 00:10:20.460
Keith Jansa: The… and there was also recognition and acknowledgement within the workshop that when we talk about interoperability, many…

61
00:10:20.620 --> 00:10:22.540
Keith Jansa: people…

62
00:10:22.830 --> 00:10:39.130
Keith Jansa: simply think technical, like the technical interoperability between systems. But it's much more than that. Policy interoperability is just as important in order to set the appropriate controls.

63
00:10:39.160 --> 00:10:51.999
Keith Jansa: That are necessary and to shape things like, the sovereignty, the economics, and the rights associated with the way in which systems then are controlled and interoperate together.

64
00:10:52.440 --> 00:11:05.359
Keith Jansa: There was also a commentary within that workshop around how empires once built railways, and today they build algorithms, highlighting that in the current

65
00:11:05.710 --> 00:11:15.249
Keith Jansa: states, there is, geopolitical tensions, that are very much,

66
00:11:15.960 --> 00:11:25.080
Keith Jansa: Causing, concern, challenges, perhaps opportunities, with respect to how, countries are…

67
00:11:25.500 --> 00:11:31.220
Keith Jansa: Defining their own, sovereign control and serving their economy.

68
00:11:31.330 --> 00:11:37.060
Keith Jansa: The key question, though, is not whether systems can interoperate. We heard quite…

69
00:11:37.410 --> 00:11:42.240
Keith Jansa: Regularly in the workshop that the technology is there.

70
00:11:42.710 --> 00:11:48.370
Keith Jansa: The question is really under whose laws, values, and governance, in order to have

71
00:11:48.810 --> 00:11:52.730
Keith Jansa: In order to trust, and ultimately have confidence in these systems.

72
00:11:53.810 --> 00:12:09.190
Keith Jansa: Now, the model in the EU, which many of you may be aware, is very much what I would refer to as top-down. It's a structured regulatory environment. You have the EIDAS for identity, you have an AI Act, you have a Digital Services Act.

73
00:12:09.250 --> 00:12:15.129
Keith Jansa: All of this setting the rules and governance associated to how

74
00:12:15.210 --> 00:12:22.020
Keith Jansa: Europe, leverages, uses, and governs digital technologies.

75
00:12:22.190 --> 00:12:30.620
Keith Jansa: in Canada, and it is much more of a decentralized pan-Canadian, given that the Federation of

76
00:12:30.880 --> 00:12:35.289
Keith Jansa: Canada, with the provinces and territories, coupled with the federal government.

77
00:12:35.480 --> 00:12:40.139
Keith Jansa: A consensus-driven approach that's rooted in that federation.

78
00:12:40.320 --> 00:12:59.579
Keith Jansa: And so, recognizing that different jurisdictions, like provincial jurisdiction has certain accountabilities, authorities, and responsibilities and powers in comparison to the federal government, and when it comes to things like identity, and credentials as to…

79
00:12:59.640 --> 00:13:04.339
Keith Jansa: who I am those, of course, cross,

80
00:13:04.670 --> 00:13:08.310
Keith Jansa: Those… those jurisdictions, federally and provincially.

81
00:13:08.680 --> 00:13:23.229
Keith Jansa: Now, the workshop also emphasized, with respect to these governance models, even though one is top-down and bottom-up, like I said earlier, with respect to the model law, the UN model law around digital identity and trust services.

82
00:13:23.230 --> 00:13:34.879
Keith Jansa: that those controls, when looked at with respect to the regulatory environment in the EU, or the more consensus-driven policy approach in Canada, that there is alignment.

83
00:13:35.130 --> 00:13:54.570
Keith Jansa: And so, given that alignment and toward those outcomes of, you know, privacy-preserving, interoperable approaches, the workshop participants very much emphasized, reciprocity, transparency, and domestic accountability as foundational with respect to how Canada and the EU ultimately crave for interoperability.

84
00:13:54.570 --> 00:13:58.290
Keith Jansa: Between, their respective policies.

85
00:13:58.350 --> 00:14:02.089
Keith Jansa: As well as, the technologies themselves.

86
00:14:03.120 --> 00:14:17.110
Keith Jansa: Now, that workshop also emphasized that we needed to take a use case-driven approach, coupled with that study that I mentioned earlier, really looking at pilots as a… as…

87
00:14:17.410 --> 00:14:22.740
Keith Jansa: An opportunity to have, living laboratories for interoperability, to…

88
00:14:22.860 --> 00:14:34.389
Keith Jansa: create the necessary market conditions in both jurisdictions to enable for this interoperability, but to also test, and to, I would say, stress test

89
00:14:34.490 --> 00:14:39.810
Keith Jansa: The appropriate… the governance in order to have trust between jurisdictions.

90
00:14:40.070 --> 00:14:51.629
Keith Jansa: Some high-value, pilot areas that were discussed, were everything from mobile driver's license, travel credentials, education, professional qualifications. At the end of the day.

91
00:14:51.970 --> 00:15:11.749
Keith Jansa: within that workshop, there was a convergence around B2B and workplace applications as being the least contentious and the most impactful early pilots, as its focus is on the business-to-business relationship in comparison to that with a citizen.

92
00:15:11.750 --> 00:15:21.839
Keith Jansa: And within the Canadian context, Digital identity, is quite polarized and quite contentious in that there are…

93
00:15:21.960 --> 00:15:29.339
Keith Jansa: Narratives and dialogues that suggest that digital identity does not, in fact, provide

94
00:15:29.500 --> 00:15:35.159
Keith Jansa: For, privacy, that it would result in a surveillance state.

95
00:15:35.230 --> 00:15:48.079
Keith Jansa: And that the behaviors of Canadians would be, attuned by, you know, by government will, and, you know, the notion of, you know, chips installed in the back of your

96
00:15:48.080 --> 00:15:58.099
Keith Jansa: your neck. So, this perception, this negative connotation associated to digital identity in Canada certainly, creates pause.

97
00:15:58.250 --> 00:16:16.600
Keith Jansa: with respect to how, you can instill digital trust and confidence within the public around the use of these technologies. Now, certainly, I'm speaking to a group here that is very familiar with, understands, recognizes what digital identity is.

98
00:16:17.010 --> 00:16:30.840
Keith Jansa: And what it does, in that it actually, preserves privacy, or in fact, creates even more confidence with respect to the privacy of a person, in that you can have, you know, zero knowledge

99
00:16:31.040 --> 00:16:49.590
Keith Jansa: associated with, you know, I'm not showing my driver's license with all that information in order to purchase alcohol. I'm only providing whether I'm of the age of majority, so data minimization, these kinds of approaches to technology that can further preserve privacy.

100
00:16:49.660 --> 00:16:52.620
Keith Jansa: That all said, given the context in Canada.

101
00:16:52.710 --> 00:17:03.870
Keith Jansa: being different than that in Europe, where digital identity is literally listed in the IDAS as, you know, it's digital identity legislation, having legislation like that in Canada

102
00:17:05.010 --> 00:17:12.510
Keith Jansa: With the public, concerns associated would just never… would never… would never happen.

103
00:17:13.310 --> 00:17:16.589
Keith Jansa: Now, that all said, what also was recognized is that

104
00:17:16.770 --> 00:17:23.619
Keith Jansa: given the digital identity, legislation and framework in Europe.

105
00:17:24.060 --> 00:17:29.279
Keith Jansa: Driver's licenses aren't regarded as a form of identity.

106
00:17:29.500 --> 00:17:31.849
Keith Jansa: It's a privilege to drive a vehicle.

107
00:17:32.090 --> 00:17:37.690
Keith Jansa: In Canada, of course, we recognize driver's license as a form of identity.

108
00:17:38.570 --> 00:17:51.070
Keith Jansa: And what's important to recognize there is that in the context of having interoperability between these two jurisdictions, the recognition of a driver's license in that way in Europe

109
00:17:51.400 --> 00:17:53.750
Keith Jansa: Is… is a foreign concept.

110
00:17:53.910 --> 00:18:01.209
Keith Jansa: Given that that's not the way in which those, Those cards are recognized.

111
00:18:01.320 --> 00:18:20.939
Keith Jansa: So, in turn, again, looking at B2B as an opportunity to further, establish pilots that can stress test the governance mechanism and ultimately the trust, but recognizing at the same time that there are areas in which there's a difference in how

112
00:18:20.970 --> 00:18:27.460
Keith Jansa: How these… Two jurisdictions assure that a person is who they say they are.

113
00:18:29.110 --> 00:18:40.330
Keith Jansa: And when it comes to the mutual recognition and legal pathways, there is a call, and was a call, for a formal Canada-EU-Canada mutual recognition agreement grounded in that UN model law.

114
00:18:40.680 --> 00:18:51.130
Keith Jansa: Recognizing that both approaches, whether it's policy-driven in Canada, consensus-based, or top-down in the EU, that there is alignment with that to the model law.

115
00:18:51.290 --> 00:19:08.889
Keith Jansa: And the aim, ultimately, would be to recognize the legal effect of identity systems and trust services across these jurisdictions, meaning that if I'm issuing a credential in Canada, that it ultimately can be accepted as having the same legal effect in Europe as it does in Canada.

116
00:19:09.450 --> 00:19:11.379
Keith Jansa: And then vice versa.

117
00:19:11.820 --> 00:19:27.989
Keith Jansa: And by having this formal mutual recognition agreement coupled with the legal effects around the identity systems, we can provide legal certainty around the acceptance of these credentials and electronic signatures that cross borders.

118
00:19:29.520 --> 00:19:38.669
Keith Jansa: Conformity assessment and assurance mapping was also a fundamental piece of the conversation, and again, included in the study, that

119
00:19:38.670 --> 00:19:56.360
Keith Jansa: there is a need to recognize third-party evaluators across both regions. With respect to the technology, and as you look to conformity assessment, in terms of having third-party, you know, independent assessments and certifications associated with that technology, the management controls of an organization and certifying

120
00:19:56.360 --> 00:19:58.829
Keith Jansa: Those with respect to establishing trust.

121
00:19:58.870 --> 00:20:09.829
Keith Jansa: In the ecosystem, in the way organizations are governing those technologies, was a conversation and dialogue that also, took place within the workshop.

122
00:20:10.030 --> 00:20:29.569
Keith Jansa: and is articulated in the study, around establishing common assurance mapping between Canada's national standard system of leveraging consensus-based national standards with certification and verification associated with those controls with that of the EU AI DAS.

123
00:20:29.710 --> 00:20:34.949
Keith Jansa: And that the conformity assessment could ensure transparency and regulatory confidence in public trust.

124
00:20:35.200 --> 00:20:47.670
Keith Jansa: So, in turn, what this would mean in establishing mutual recognition of certificates that are issued in Canada or in the EU with respect to the way a technology is governed, used.

125
00:20:47.700 --> 00:20:56.639
Keith Jansa: And or used, that, that certificate would have, acceptance within both regions.

126
00:20:56.680 --> 00:21:07.760
Keith Jansa: So in other words, I could certify in Canada or verify in Canada through an independent third party, and that would be accepted in Europe. Likewise, in Europe, you could do

127
00:21:07.760 --> 00:21:18.890
Keith Jansa: to the European model, and have it accepted into Canada through a mutual recognition agreement that establishes the conformity assessment mechanisms and mapping to assure that trust.

128
00:21:19.740 --> 00:21:25.780
Keith Jansa: At the same time, though, as I mentioned, mutual recognition, we're recognizing the other

129
00:21:26.130 --> 00:21:37.750
Keith Jansa: jurisdiction's rulebook. So, in other words, Europe with EIDAS, any organization that effectively is in compliance and conformity with that through those rules, Canada would accept.

130
00:21:37.810 --> 00:21:51.289
Keith Jansa: And likewise, in the Canadian market, for organizations that are in compliance with the consensus-based policy standards within Canada, and the certifications in relation, that Europe would accept it.

131
00:21:52.470 --> 00:21:56.089
Keith Jansa: That does challenge, in part.

132
00:21:56.360 --> 00:22:03.820
Keith Jansa: Organizations that are looking to do business across borders, In that, I… would…

133
00:22:04.270 --> 00:22:10.490
Keith Jansa: need to have Canadian organizations using European rules in order to have

134
00:22:10.720 --> 00:22:24.209
Keith Jansa: In order to have, access to that market and vice versa. And so there was this core principle also established in the study and discussed during the workshop around functional equivalence. So it's one thing to have mutual recognition.

135
00:22:24.420 --> 00:22:37.310
Keith Jansa: But mutual recognition doesn't require identical systems, only equivalent assurance and reliability. So by applying functional equivalence, it can provide a practical alternative to regulatory harmonization. In other words.

136
00:22:37.690 --> 00:22:49.550
Keith Jansa: Europe can use their system, Canada can use their system, and we can recognize those systems and the results of those systems in order to have cross-border flow and trust with respect to credentials and trust services.

137
00:22:50.160 --> 00:23:02.110
Keith Jansa: What that also does is preserves the sovereignty of both jurisdictions. That I don't need to adjust my rulebook in order to, ensure that I can continue to have access to your market.

138
00:23:02.350 --> 00:23:05.490
Keith Jansa: And vice versa for the other jurisdiction.

139
00:23:05.840 --> 00:23:12.489
Keith Jansa: And this principle effectively underpins the feasibility of achieving that Canada-EU mutual trust arrangement.

140
00:23:12.620 --> 00:23:27.259
Keith Jansa: Otherwise, we would need to fundamentally adjust our approaches to governance and the way in which we use technology in order to have access to a market like Europe and vice versa.

141
00:23:28.840 --> 00:23:30.070
Keith Jansa: And so…

142
00:23:30.550 --> 00:23:43.799
Keith Jansa: In the workshop, and what was very clear, and what resonated, was the fact that technical trust in and of itself, so the interoperability between the systems itself, is insufficient.

143
00:23:43.920 --> 00:23:54.729
Keith Jansa: that there is a social trust that's essential, and certainly in the Canadian context, as I mentioned, with respect to the negative connotation, just using digital and identity as two words combined.

144
00:23:54.840 --> 00:23:57.399
Keith Jansa: that challenge in Canada.

145
00:23:57.530 --> 00:24:03.879
Keith Jansa: There are also concerns that were raised with respect to the misinformation and the narrative's framing digital identity as that threat.

146
00:24:04.510 --> 00:24:16.220
Keith Jansa: And that success in the Canadian context, unlike the European one, where it's mandatory, would require voluntary participation, transparency, audibility, and meaningful consent.

147
00:24:17.550 --> 00:24:34.439
Keith Jansa: There were international lessons and risks also discussed. The Kenya example was used as failed governance despite the technology. There was an emphasis around public engagement and democratic legitimacy of credentials.

148
00:24:34.490 --> 00:24:43.630
Keith Jansa: And that Canada's federated standards-based model is seen as inclusive and resilient, as it is an open, transparent process where

149
00:24:43.780 --> 00:24:51.520
Keith Jansa: Stakeholders can engage, and the public can engage in the establishment of the rules that ultimately get codified.

150
00:24:54.070 --> 00:25:08.469
Keith Jansa: In terms of the policy and the technical and the legal alignment, so interoperability does require, as I mentioned earlier, it's not just a technical conversation, it is a policy one, it is a legislative one, standards and engagement, it requires

151
00:25:08.570 --> 00:25:14.950
Keith Jansa: Simultaneous progress in these areas in order to achieve mutual recognition and equivalence.

152
00:25:15.360 --> 00:25:31.880
Keith Jansa: There was also, through this workshop, proposed creation of a joint Canada-EU working group that would focus on the legal and technical alignment, create for those conditions to establish the mutual recognition agreement, as well as the appropriate equivalents.

153
00:25:32.100 --> 00:25:45.520
Keith Jansa: And then that there would be shared middleware layer proposed around that functional equivalence, around the processes and the assurance, whether it's issuing a certificate or a verification statement associated to a technology or a business.

154
00:25:47.280 --> 00:25:49.460
Keith Jansa: What was also articulated.

155
00:25:49.550 --> 00:25:53.239
Keith Jansa: In the workshop, and as well in the study, is that we're not starting from scratch.

156
00:25:53.290 --> 00:26:10.049
Keith Jansa: Canada and the EU already have a trusted trade relationship. We have the CETA trade agreement between Canada and the EU, and there is a protocol within the trade agreement that is around conformity assessment.

157
00:26:11.000 --> 00:26:17.459
Keith Jansa: That conformity assessment protocol, Already permits for a certificate to be issued?

158
00:26:17.790 --> 00:26:20.510
Keith Jansa: Primarily for physical goods, like toys.

159
00:26:20.630 --> 00:26:27.069
Keith Jansa: To be recognized in the other person's jurisdiction, using what's in your jurisdiction as the rulebook.

160
00:26:27.760 --> 00:26:28.790
Keith Jansa: Now…

161
00:26:29.430 --> 00:26:45.440
Keith Jansa: what is being proposed is that we extend those physical goods to add digital credentials and trust services within that conformity assessment protocol, meaning that if I'm using a standard in Canada, or using a standard in the EU, or through its legislation.

162
00:26:45.460 --> 00:26:53.580
Keith Jansa: That conformity assessment with respect to a third party issuing a certificate or a verification statement, that it'd ultimately be accepted in the other jurisdiction.

163
00:26:53.660 --> 00:27:04.069
Keith Jansa: This would ground the digital trust framework in the way the architecture of trade, and would also reduce compliance costs, in that I don't need to certify against both rulebooks.

164
00:27:04.190 --> 00:27:11.840
Keith Jansa: And then it also extends the logic of that product equivalence that currently exists into the digital services domain.

165
00:27:12.950 --> 00:27:32.000
Keith Jansa: In terms of the actual technical collaboration required here in the priority areas is mapping assurance levels. So there is assurance levels in the EU, there is comparative ones in Canada, there needs to be a crosswalk between those in order to establish what levels of assurance and trust are necessary for having the acceptance of

166
00:27:32.000 --> 00:27:35.260
Keith Jansa: Credentials and the use of, trust services.

167
00:27:35.580 --> 00:27:41.800
Keith Jansa: There's also a recognition that there needs to be an approach to technical collaboration that, that…

168
00:27:42.000 --> 00:27:51.889
Keith Jansa: maintains continuous updates as technologies evolve, and the risk landscape changes. That risk can be technical, that risk can also be geopolitical.

169
00:27:52.080 --> 00:28:01.729
Keith Jansa: And then also recognizing that there needs to be a shared operational pathway for validation, credential exchange, and verification, based on

170
00:28:01.910 --> 00:28:05.320
Keith Jansa: ISO 17029 methodology, so…

171
00:28:05.710 --> 00:28:17.140
Keith Jansa: In large respects, with respect to pilots that have occurred in Canada, even within the EU, it largely recognizes the conformity assessment schemes of 17025.

172
00:28:17.310 --> 00:28:23.769
Keith Jansa: 17025 of ISON IAC focuses primarily on goods and services.

173
00:28:24.110 --> 00:28:37.069
Keith Jansa: And it is referred primarily as product certification, whereas 17029 focuses on validation and verification, which could look at

174
00:28:37.140 --> 00:28:44.780
Keith Jansa: Goods and services, but it can also look at, an organization's, controls.

175
00:28:44.860 --> 00:29:04.689
Keith Jansa: And it's management, and issuing a statement of verification, because one thing that's very clear with respect to product certification, when you look at your toaster and you see that mark of conformity, it doesn't say much, right? It's a physical product, you see this mark, there's some, you know, perhaps some people naive as to what it means, but it looks like it's been checked.

176
00:29:04.840 --> 00:29:21.480
Keith Jansa: In the digital context where there's a lower level of trust in relation to these technologies, there needs to be a more informative way to the public to indicate what that mark means. And so 17029 issues statements of verification. Those statements of verification

177
00:29:21.510 --> 00:29:31.079
Keith Jansa: Verify digital claims that an organization makes with respect to its controls, with respect to the way in which it governs its technology and manages them, with how they use them.

178
00:29:31.240 --> 00:29:47.129
Keith Jansa: And that information can be extremely important to be able to address misinformation, disinformation in the market, and also serve as a tool to instill confidence and trust beyond what a mark of conformity can do in 17065, given that

